1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:02,640
Wealth. Fame. Power.

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:04,577
Gold Roger, the King of the Pirates,

3
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:07,197
attained this and everything
else the world had to offer,

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,456
and his dying words drove
countless souls to the seas.

5
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:11,955
You want my treasure?

6
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:13,420
You can have it.

7
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,600
I left everything I gathered
together in one place.

8
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,060
Now you just have to find it.

9
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:24,237
These words lured men to
the Grand Line in pursuit

10
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,757
of dreams greater than
they'd ever dared to imagine.

11
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,517
This is the time known
as the Great Pirate Era.

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:35,140
Come aboard and bring along
all your hopes and dreams.

13
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:40,440
Together we will find everything
that we're looking for!

14
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,700
One piece.

15
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:52,697
Compass left behind,
it'll only slow us down.

16
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:58,417
Your heart will be your guide,
raise the sails and take the helm.

17
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,117
That legendary place?

18
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,817
That the end of the map reveals.

19
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,717
Is only legendary?

20
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:10,117
Until someone proves it real.

21
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,557
Through it all!

22
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,477
Through all the trouble and times!

23
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,817
Through the heartache
and through the pain!

24
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:21,477
Know that I will be there to stand by you.

25
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,180
Swack, I know you'll stand by me!

26
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:30,417
So come aboard and bring along
all your hopes and dreams.

27
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:35,897
Together we will find everything
that we're looking for.

28
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:42,400
There's always room for you
if you want to be my friend.

29
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:44,657
We are.

30
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:45,897
We are.

31
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:46,810
On the cruise.

32
00:01:46,893 --> 00:01:49,700
We are.

33
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:01,391
There's absolutely no way we'll make
it to the Grand Line in this condition.

34
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:07,174
You're right, we're going to
need a whole lot more meat.

35
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:10,597
Eating fruit every day is for the birds.

36
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:12,268
We'll also need some booze.

37
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:14,060
Shut up, you boneheads!

38
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:17,797
That's not what I mean.

39
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:19,617
The Grand Line's a dangerous place.

40
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:21,617
We'll need more than beer
and meat to survive there.

41
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,437
Not to mention the place is crawling with

42
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,357
pirates who are also in
search of the one piece.

43
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:27,917
And they all have ships that
are way better than this!

44
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,397
And we don't have a crew.

45
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,557
We don't have a crew.

46
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:33,657
We don't have any equipment or
even a real ship for that matter.

47
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:36,477
There is no way we'll
ever make it like this.

48
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:39,857
Yep, we'll need a cook and maybe
a musician to keep us entertained.

49
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:41,040
A musician?

50
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:45,030
So stupid.

51
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:49,617
A little to the south of here is an
island with some settlements on it.

52
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:52,217
And it looks like we might
be able to get our ship.

53
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:53,757
And some of the supplies we need there.

54
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:55,597
Fresh meat at last.

55
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,340
Lots of booze as well.

56
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:02,797
Sound the alarm.

57
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:03,797
They're here.

58
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:06,177
They're here and they're
gonna kill us all.

59
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:07,160
Oh no.

60
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:09,580
Pirates, pirates.

61
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:11,577
Pirates are attacking.

62
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:12,560
Run.

63
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,637
Everybody run for your lives.

64
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:18,180
Pirates, pirates. Pirates!

65
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:19,380
Everybody run!

66
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:20,720
Pirates are coming!

67
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:24,142
Their bloodlust is unquenchable!

68
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:29,100
We're doomed.

69
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,417
The pirates are...

70
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:34,800
uh... LIE!

71
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:39,420
That hurt!

72
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:42,820
You little liar!

73
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,178
You're not gonna get away this time!

74
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:49,360
Wait, wait, wait!

75
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:50,870
Now run!

76
00:03:57,860 --> 00:03:59,340
Just try and catch me!

77
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:00,920
Come on, slowpokes!

78
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:06,580
Ah, man.

79
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:08,646
Looks like he got away again.

80
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,140
That kid's a Newgate troublemaker.

81
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,737
He'd better hope I
never get a hold of him.

82
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:16,644
Looks like nobody's getting
a hold of him today.

83
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,780
Yet another job well done.

84
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:32,897
It is my solemn duty to send the wind of
excitement through this boring old village.

85
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:34,160
There he is!

86
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:36,070
Hmm?

87
00:04:36,153 --> 00:04:37,364
Hey, you guys, what's going on?

88
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:39,715
I bid you good morning, Captain Usopp!

89
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:43,531
Fear not, your loyal crew, the
Usopp pirates, have arrived.

90
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:47,740
Pepper! Carrot! It's just you two?

91
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:48,750
Where's Onion?

92
00:04:48,833 --> 00:04:50,304
Have you seen him around today?

93
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,960
Sound the alarm! They're
here! They're here!

94
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:00,040
They're here and they're
gonna kill us all!

95
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:01,560
Hey, it's Onion.

96
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:03,897
I wonder what he's freaking out about.

97
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:05,000
Pirates! Pirates!

98
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:05,950
They're here!

99
00:05:06,033 --> 00:05:06,863
Run for your lives!

100
00:05:06,946 --> 00:05:07,776
Pirates!

101
00:05:07,859 --> 00:05:08,689
We don't stand a chance!

102
00:05:08,772 --> 00:05:09,602
Bloodthirsty murderers!

103
00:05:09,685 --> 00:05:11,445
If Lincoln run, everybody run!

104
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:13,520
Oh, stop playing.

105
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:17,360
I'm not playing.

106
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:19,620
It's for real?

107
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,158
Yes, it's for real.

108
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:22,300
Pirates are here.

109
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:24,430
Gotta go.

110
00:05:24,513 --> 00:05:27,080
Time for me to have my snack. You chicken.

111
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:31,100
Actually, I'm sick. Yeah, I have.

112
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:33,475
I'll die if I don't eat my snackiosis.

113
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:35,660
You liar.

114
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:38,517
You always told us that
you wanted to become

115
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,657
a real pirate someday
and now's our chance.

116
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:42,260
Yeah! What's with you?

117
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:44,777
What kind of a pirate is
afraid of other pirates?

118
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,978
If I recall correctly, it
was Buggy the Clown's mark.

119
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:50,340
Buggy the Clown?

120
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:55,740
I think I'll just...

121
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:58,540
But I only saw two tiny boats.

122
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:01,021
And there were just three people.

123
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,660
It's go time, brave Usa pirates!

124
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:07,822
We do have a village to protect.

125
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:10,240
Let's go, men! Move out!

126
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:13,210
Sir!

127
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:24,780
Feels good to be on dry land again.

128
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:27,937
There's a village around
here somewhere, right?

129
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:28,850
Yeah.

130
00:06:28,933 --> 00:06:30,400
It looks like a small one, though.

131
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:32,070
Ooh, ooh!

132
00:06:32,153 --> 00:06:33,117
Then there's meat!

133
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:34,030
There's meat!

134
00:06:34,113 --> 00:06:36,517
There has to be a place to
eat meat in this village!

135
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:39,306
Just forget about meat
for a minute, would ya?

136
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:45,677
Careful, we're being watched.
Huh? By who? Where are they?

137
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:46,590
They got meat?

138
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:47,630
Look out!

139
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,500
That's so cool!

140
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,328
Is this supposed to be
when we're impressed?

141
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:09,217
I am Usopp, leader of the invincible nation
of pirates who reign over this island.

142
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:13,337
You stand before a living legend, the
most revered pirate who ever lived!

143
00:07:13,420 --> 00:07:15,180
I am the great Captain Usopp!

144
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:21,337
Surely you know that plotting an attack
on this island would mean certain death?

145
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:25,891
My army of 80 million super mean pirate
soldiers would squash you like bugs!

146
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:27,730
Awesome!

147
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:34,480
You expect us to believe that? No!

148
00:07:34,780 --> 00:07:35,680
She knows!

149
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:37,530
Yep.

150
00:07:37,613 --> 00:07:38,443
Well, now I know.

151
00:07:38,526 --> 00:07:40,117
How could I have been so dumb?

152
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,837
I practically told her I was lying!

153
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:43,861
I'm such an idiot, I'm so stupid!

154
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:46,970
What?

155
00:07:47,053 --> 00:07:47,883
He was lying?

156
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:49,962
Curse you, you crafty tactician!

157
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:52,377
Eighty million may have
been an exaggeration,

158
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:55,107
but I have a great many
men under my control!

159
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:03,200
My guess is three.

160
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:05,870
Yeah!

161
00:08:05,953 --> 00:08:06,783
She does know!

162
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:12,900
Hey, where are you guys going? Come back!

163
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:17,660
That's pretty cool!

164
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:21,732
Shut your mouth, or I'll shut it for you!

165
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:24,371
I'm a proud man, and I won't stand for it!

166
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:28,781
This is your last warning, or you'll
get to see why they call me...

167
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:32,160
Proud Usopp, and not pushover Usopp.

168
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:34,320
Is that clear?

169
00:08:35,420 --> 00:08:38,337
You've already seen for yourself
just how good I am with this thing.

170
00:08:38,420 --> 00:08:41,361
So, you know I can rival
anyone carrying a pistol!

171
00:08:47,620 --> 00:08:50,738
Now that you've drawn your
pistol, are you willing...

172
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:51,850
to use it?

173
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:56,477
Guns aren't for threats,
they're for actions.

174
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:58,560
Are you willing to risk your life?

175
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:17,817
Real pirates definitely
have a lot more punch behind

176
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:20,217
their words because they
can actually back them up.

177
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:21,440
That's so cool.

178
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:29,320
I stole that. Huh?

179
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:33,209
I stole those words from
Red-Haired Shanks, a pirate I know.

180
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:34,570
What?

181
00:09:34,653 --> 00:09:35,877
You know Red-Haired Shanks?

182
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:37,237
You really are a real pirate.

183
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:38,737
Wait, you've got to be lying.

184
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:40,480
Do you really know him? Yep.

185
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:42,320
I know your dad, too.

186
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:44,520
His name's Yasop, right? Huh?

187
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:53,750
You're right.

188
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
My old man's name is Yasop.

189
00:09:57,220 --> 00:09:58,220
But how...

190
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:01,581
How in the world do you know him?

191
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:12,319
This is good, huh?

192
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:17,085
So I met your dad when
I was just a little kid.

193
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:19,537
Hold on, does my old
man really know Shanks?

194
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:20,450
Know him.

195
00:10:20,533 --> 00:10:22,497
When I met your dad, he
was part of Shanks' crew,

196
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:24,517
so they were probably pretty good friends.

197
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:25,960
I can't believe it!

198
00:10:26,140 --> 00:10:29,157
My old man's sailing the high
seas with famous pirates.

199
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:33,240
I haven't heard from him in a while,
but I bet he's still with them.

200
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:35,922
He was a heck of a sharpshooter.

201
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:37,640
I'd keep him on my crew.

202
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:55,980
Whoa! Good shot!

203
00:10:57,220 --> 00:10:58,257
Eh, that's nothing.

204
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:01,575
I could shoot an ant between
the eyes from across town.

205
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:07,180
You know something, Luffy?

206
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:11,137
I got a kid named Usopp, and
he's just about your age.

207
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:13,897
You told me about him
already, a hundred times.

208
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:14,810
A hundred?

209
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:17,860
Well, too bad.

210
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:19,760
I'm telling you again.

211
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:24,180
It broke my heart to
leave, but I had to go.

212
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:29,240
You see, Luffy, when the pirate flag
beckons, you must hate its call.

213
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:42,367
He used to go on and on
and on about his son.

214
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:44,857
I think I heard enough about
you to make my ears bleed.

215
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:47,297
And let me tell you,
he was a great pirate.

216
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:48,240
Yeah, really?

217
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:50,780
I sure am proud of my old man.

218
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:53,842
He leapt into a pirate's
life on the endless sea.

219
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:57,079
And chose a life of real adventure.

220
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,460
Maybe you can help us.

221
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:03,197
Do you know someone who could
provide us with a decent ship,

222
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:06,398
or even anyone who knows
how to sail for that matter?

223
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:08,840
Well, it's a pretty small village.

224
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:12,357
Sorry, but I just don't think
we'll be able to help you.

225
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:14,146
What about that huge mansion?

226
00:12:14,440 --> 00:12:16,734
The one on top of that hill over there.

227
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:18,900
Hey, do you know the Moosop?

228
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:22,257
With all that money, surely
they can help us get our boat.

229
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:23,820
You stay away from there!

230
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:25,790
Ah!

231
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:29,495
Ah, I just remembered something
that I really gotta do.

232
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:34,818
Everybody knows me at this place,
so stay and eat as much as you want.

233
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:36,720
Well, I gotta go!

234
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:42,326
Why was he in such a
hurry to get out of here?

235
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:48,717
Are you sure this is
the place they took him?

236
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:50,617
Yeah, those terrible
pirates dragged him inside,

237
00:12:50,700 --> 00:12:52,317
and now it's up to us to save our captain.

238
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:53,737
But guys, those are real pirates.

239
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:55,377
What if they've grilled
and eaten him already?

240
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:58,597
It's grizzly old witch hag to
eat their victims, not pirates!

241
00:12:58,680 --> 00:13:01,837
This is going to be the most brutal
and epic battle of our lives.

242
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:04,517
We must show no mercy because
they'll never show it to us.

243
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:05,430
Hmm.

244
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:09,020
The Usopp pirates have arrived!

245
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:10,720
Who are these kids?

246
00:13:10,980 --> 00:13:13,240
Captain Usopp is gone. Did they?

247
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:15,780
Vice up, you filthy pirates!

248
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:17,717
What have you done with
our beloved captain?

249
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:18,630
Tell us now!

250
00:13:18,713 --> 00:13:19,840
Yeah, tell us now!

251
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:22,860
That meat sure was good.

252
00:13:23,540 --> 00:13:24,380
What meat?

253
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:26,307
These pirates are cannibals!

254
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:29,980
What have you done, you savages?

255
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:32,860
If you're looking for your captain...

256
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:35,940
we ate him.

257
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:39,960
Ah, careful!

258
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,876
What are you punks looking at me for?

259
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:47,542
You just had to say
something stupid, didn't you?

260
00:13:58,400 --> 00:14:00,440
You wish to see Usopp.

261
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:02,960
Don't start this again.

262
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:04,840
May I see him?

263
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:06,480
I like talking to him.

264
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:08,820
No, ma 'am.

265
00:14:09,180 --> 00:14:11,709
That man is without a
doubt a chronic liar.

266
00:14:12,020 --> 00:14:13,857
For someone in your weakened state,

267
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:17,097
Usopp's outlandish stories
might be too much to handle.

268
00:14:17,180 --> 00:14:21,417
Your late parents instructed me to protect
you to the fullest of my abilities.

269
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:24,997
It would be against their wishes
for you to see him, Miss Kaya.

270
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:27,786
Now then, I'll leave
your medicine right here.

271
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:29,429
Please be sure to take it.

272
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:35,710
Killjoy.

273
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:37,100
Killjoy will do.

274
00:14:37,420 --> 00:14:39,655
A name I'll bear if you are kept safe.

275
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:53,320
Usopp, you came! Of course. How are you?

276
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:54,357
You seem a little down.

277
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:55,860
I guess I am.

278
00:14:56,060 --> 00:14:57,417
I'm tired of talking like this.

279
00:14:57,500 --> 00:15:00,317
I'd like to invite you
inside like a proper guest.

280
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,635
But there's just no way
Clawador would ever allow that.

281
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:05,685
He's not a bad person, though.

282
00:15:05,880 --> 00:15:06,757
Don't sweat it.

283
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:10,897
Remember, I'm a brave warrior of the sea
who likes endless oceans and the open sky.

284
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:12,697
Houses weren't made for rogues like me.

285
00:15:12,780 --> 00:15:15,133
I'd probably just feel cramped in there.

286
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,535
So what adventure are you
going to tell me about today?

287
00:15:19,080 --> 00:15:19,910
Hmm.

288
00:15:19,993 --> 00:15:20,823
Well, let's see.

289
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:22,060
Oh, I know.

290
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:24,517
What about the story of me
fighting a giant goldfish

291
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:26,937
at the South Pole when I
was only five years old.

292
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:27,920
A goldfish?

293
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:29,500
A giant goldfish.

294
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:32,777
What first astounded me was
the immense size of its poop.

295
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:36,197
It was so big that when I landed on
it, I thought it was solid ground.

296
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:37,537
It looked like an island.

297
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:39,520
Ha ha ha ha!

298
00:15:52,820 --> 00:15:53,650
Whoa!

299
00:15:53,733 --> 00:15:54,780
This place is huge!

300
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:58,957
And if our captain ran out for this hour,
then this is the place he's gonna be.

301
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:01,805
Oh, what in the world
does Usopp come here for?

302
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:04,780
He comes to lie. To lie? That's terrible.

303
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:06,158
He's not a bad guy.

304
00:16:06,260 --> 00:16:07,495
He's great, isn't he?

305
00:16:08,040 --> 00:16:09,160
Yeah, he's awesome.

306
00:16:09,420 --> 00:16:10,940
How is a liar awesome?

307
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:13,580
Well, Kaya,

308
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:17,157
the girl who lives there has been
in a terrible depression that's kept

309
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:21,260
her weak and in bed since her
parents got sick and died a year ago.

310
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:26,460
She's very rich, but all that money
couldn't help her recover from her sadness.

311
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:30,897
So our captain tells us special made-up
stories to make her laugh and cheer her up.

312
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,568
Captain herself is the
best at telling lies.

313
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:39,617
Wow, that really is pretty nice of him.

314
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:40,530
Yeah!

315
00:16:40,613 --> 00:16:43,057
The best thing about him
is his mischievous side.

316
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:44,517
He's always up to something.

317
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:46,697
I like him because he's
not afraid to be silly.

318
00:16:46,780 --> 00:16:48,898
And I like the captain's crazy lies.

319
00:16:49,140 --> 00:16:52,257
So when he tells her those
stories, he makes her feel better?

320
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:54,580
Yep, lots better. Great!

321
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:57,805
I think we should ask
her for a ship after all!

322
00:16:58,280 --> 00:16:59,751
Getting in is impossible.

323
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:02,797
Those guards at the gate
don't let anyone through.

324
00:17:02,880 --> 00:17:04,320
Oh, where's Luffy?

325
00:17:04,820 --> 00:17:05,920
I'll go ask her!

326
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:08,640
No, you're not going to...

327
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:14,700
Pardon the intrusion!

328
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:23,777
So after that terrifying and
traumatic little incident,

329
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:27,360
we escaped into the sky riding
on a giant slingshot ball!

330
00:17:28,980 --> 00:17:29,810
What's that?

331
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:32,130
Yahoo!

332
00:17:39,780 --> 00:17:41,000
That worked well.

333
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:42,860
My goodness, are you okay?

334
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:45,260
Ah, don't mind them, Kaya.

335
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:46,597
This happens to me all the time.

336
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:48,557
That's just the price of
being a famous adventurer.

337
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:52,357
They're just new recruits who heard about
me and came to join the Usoppirates.

338
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:53,620
Nope, not me.

339
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:54,977
I came here to see you.

340
00:17:55,060 --> 00:17:56,707
I wanted to ask you a favor.

341
00:17:56,940 --> 00:17:58,960
A favor from me? Uh-huh.

342
00:17:59,340 --> 00:18:01,917
I was wondering if there was any
way you could help us get a boat.

343
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:02,830
You there.

344
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:05,924
What do you think you're doing?

345
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:09,290
Klahadore!

346
00:18:10,960 --> 00:18:11,790
Get out!

347
00:18:13,540 --> 00:18:16,658
I'll have you know that this
is criminal trespassing.

348
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:21,517
These people are...

349
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:22,560
Not now, Kaya.

350
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:27,231
You can tell me all about this
riffraff after they've left.

351
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:29,640
Now get out, all of you.

352
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,817
Leave now, unless you have
some business with me.

353
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:36,018
All right, do you think you
could help us get a ship?

354
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:37,260
Certainly not.

355
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:40,020
Usopp! Crap!

356
00:18:40,340 --> 00:18:42,105
You have quite the reputation.

357
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:44,674
You are very well known in the village.

358
00:18:45,640 --> 00:18:48,157
Quite impressive for
someone as young as you.

359
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:51,097
Usually people who admire
me so much call me Captain.

360
00:18:51,180 --> 00:18:53,100
So it's Captain Usopp to you.

361
00:18:54,140 --> 00:18:57,580
Your father also had something
of a reputation. What?

362
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:01,487
You'll never be anything more
than the son of a filthy pirate.

363
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:04,784
Who knows what sort of
trouble you're up to now.

364
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:09,871
All I ask is that you and your kind
leave this respectable young lady alone.

365
00:19:10,320 --> 00:19:12,000
A filthy pirate?

366
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:15,997
You and she are from completely
different worlds, and you know it.

367
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:17,397
Is it money you're after?

368
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:18,715
How much do you want?

369
00:19:19,880 --> 00:19:21,377
Seize this at once, Klahadore!

370
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:22,460
Apologize to him!

371
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:24,840
Hey, calm down, Kaya.

372
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:29,862
I don't believe there is any reason to
apologize to this savage young man, my lady.

373
00:19:30,440 --> 00:19:32,117
My goal isn't to slander.

374
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:33,817
I am merely stating the truth.

375
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:36,180
I sympathize with your situation.

376
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,637
You must hate your
great idiot of a father?

377
00:19:38,720 --> 00:19:40,657
For abandoning his family and his village.

378
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:43,211
because of his mindless lust for treasure.

379
00:19:43,660 --> 00:19:44,560
Cladador, stop!

380
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:48,060
Don't you dare insult
my father ever again!

381
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:50,790
Usopp!

382
00:19:59,540 --> 00:20:01,957
Why have you gotten so worked up, Usopp?

383
00:20:02,040 --> 00:20:05,457
It's times like these when you
should do what you do best and lie.

384
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:08,817
Tell me about how your father
is really a traveling salesman.

385
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:11,724
Or about how you aren't
really related by blood.

386
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:23,590
Captain!

387
00:20:25,960 --> 00:20:26,960
Case in point.

388
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:28,553
He resorts to violence.

389
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:31,397
I guess the apple never falls
far from the tree, does it?

390
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:32,310
Shut up!

391
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:37,097
My father listened to his heart and
chose a life of risk and adventure.

392
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:40,537
I am proud to say that my old man
is a brave warrior of the sea.

393
00:20:40,620 --> 00:20:43,417
You're right, I have been known
to exaggerate from time to time,

394
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:45,797
but I will never lie
about who my father is.

395
00:20:45,880 --> 00:20:48,240
I refuse to be ashamed of my heritage.

396
00:20:49,020 --> 00:20:50,800
I am the son of a pirate!

397
00:20:55,740 --> 00:20:56,570
Captain.

398
00:20:59,120 --> 00:21:01,944
Pirates are brave warriors
of the sea, are they?

399
00:21:03,660 --> 00:21:05,717
That's quite a twisted
way of putting things.

400
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:09,777
You don't seem to understand that you
are living proof of his savage blood!

401
00:21:09,860 --> 00:21:13,557
You run around spouting lies and use
violence when you don't get your way.

402
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:18,100
To top it off, you cozy up to the lady
of the house to steal her fortune?

403
00:21:18,680 --> 00:21:19,510
You're wrong!

404
00:21:19,740 --> 00:21:20,570
I only...

405
00:21:20,653 --> 00:21:21,897
Your father is a filthy,

406
00:21:21,980 --> 00:21:24,637
Newgate pirate Treachery
is in your blood Of course

407
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:27,437
you would have such an evil
scheme You won't let up,

408
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:28,350
will you?

409
00:21:28,433 --> 00:21:29,373
Stop it, Usopp, please!

410
00:21:30,460 --> 00:21:33,160
No more violence, I can't take it!

411
00:21:34,420 --> 00:21:36,240
Lahador isn't a bad person.

412
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:40,497
He's just, he's just trying to
do what he thinks is best for me,

413
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:42,345
and sometimes he goes too far.

414
00:21:45,700 --> 00:21:47,080
Leave this place.

415
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:49,800
You are never to come here again.

416
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:52,700
Yeah, I get it.

417
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:55,314
You don't have to tell me twice, buddy.

418
00:21:56,600 --> 00:21:58,894
This is the last you'll ever see of me.

419
00:22:01,580 --> 00:22:02,410
Usopp

420
00:22:04,020 --> 00:22:05,677
Hey, you butler jerk!

421
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:08,057
Our captain's not that kind of guy.

422
00:22:08,140 --> 00:22:09,797
You stupid jerk face.

423
00:22:09,880 --> 00:22:10,857
Jerk face.

424
00:22:10,940 --> 00:22:11,820
Jerk face.

425
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:15,484
Shut up, you don't have
a bone to pick with him!

426
00:22:18,260 --> 00:22:19,437
You're all serious.

427
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:21,497
Pull me down, pull me down.

428
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:22,837
I'm gonna kick his ass!

429
00:22:22,920 --> 00:22:25,120
You will all leave immediately!

430
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:40,157
Your meal is ready now, Miss Kaya.

431
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:41,237
Thank you.

432
00:22:41,320 --> 00:22:42,900
I don't want it.

433
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:49,757
The cook would be most
upset to hear you say that.

434
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:52,157
He works his hardest day and night.

435
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:55,358
To come up with delicious
meals that suit your needs.

436
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:02,580
Why were you so cruel to him?

437
00:23:02,840 --> 00:23:05,837
I know I was wrong to talk
to Usopp without telling you.

438
00:23:05,920 --> 00:23:07,117
I will admit that much.

439
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:09,677
And I'm very sorry for
not being honest with you.

440
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:13,054
But this situation did not
warrant that harsh treatment!

441
00:23:17,380 --> 00:23:18,600
May I sit down?

442
00:23:19,220 --> 00:23:20,220
Go ahead.

443
00:23:21,740 --> 00:23:24,957
It has been quite some time since
I first arrived at this house.

444
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:29,437
Soon it will be three years that I have
been in the service of your family!

445
00:23:29,520 --> 00:23:33,814
I will never forget what happened when
I showed up at your father's door.

446
00:23:42,860 --> 00:23:45,757
My former employers were
brutal, pitiless men.

447
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:49,797
I'd been kicked off the ship I worked
on for some small blunder I had made,?

448
00:23:49,880 --> 00:23:53,980
and with no work or money, I was on
the verge of dying like a beggar.

449
00:24:07,520 --> 00:24:10,897
As far as I know, I would have
died without your father's help.

450
00:24:10,980 --> 00:24:12,840
I owe my life to your parents.

451
00:24:13,380 --> 00:24:17,360
And now that they're gone,
I owe my life to you.

452
00:24:19,380 --> 00:24:22,777
I know that it isn't my place to
interfere with your friendships.

453
00:24:22,860 --> 00:24:27,566
I wouldn't have taken such a bold step
unless I thought it absolutely necessary.

454
00:24:27,900 --> 00:24:32,018
It's just that Usopp isn't exactly
known for his upstanding character.

455
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:33,870
You see,

456
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:35,977
if anything were to happen to you,

457
00:24:36,060 --> 00:24:38,097
it would be my fault for
not seeing to your safety,

458
00:24:38,180 --> 00:24:41,597
and I would never be able to ask
forgiveness from your parents.

459
00:24:41,680 --> 00:24:45,327
Do you think ill of me for my
childish behavior earlier today?

460
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:48,200
No, of course I don't.

461
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:51,217
I'm thankful to have you
around too, Klahadore.

462
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:53,040
But I stand by my opinion.

463
00:24:54,340 --> 00:24:56,537
Usopp is one of the nicest people I know.

464
00:24:56,620 --> 00:24:57,855
We'll see about that.

465
00:24:58,180 --> 00:25:00,297
Until then, he is still not welcome here.

466
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:01,210
Klahadore!

467
00:25:01,740 --> 00:25:02,860
You blockhead!

468
00:25:03,380 --> 00:25:05,027
Blockhead will do just fine.

469
00:25:09,260 --> 00:25:10,460
Where'd Luffy go?

470
00:25:11,180 --> 00:25:12,080
Who knows?

471
00:25:12,420 --> 00:25:14,479
He probably ran after that captain.

472
00:25:14,920 --> 00:25:16,800
I bet he's at his secret spot!

473
00:25:16,960 --> 00:25:18,440
Yeah, over by the shore!

474
00:25:18,680 --> 00:25:21,417
He always goes there when he's
gonna think something over.

475
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:24,597
Luffy can take care of himself,
but where's your other friend?

476
00:25:24,680 --> 00:25:25,840
Oh yeah, Onion!

477
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:29,297
Come to think of it, he just
up and disappears a lot.

478
00:25:29,380 --> 00:25:32,792
Yeah, and then he shows up out
of nowhere all freaked out.

479
00:25:34,440 --> 00:25:35,680
Terrible news, guys!

480
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:37,150
You see?

481
00:25:37,780 --> 00:25:38,780
Listen to me!

482
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:43,337
Some nut job is on his way here
and he only walks backwards!

483
00:25:43,420 --> 00:25:44,250
You liar!

484
00:25:44,380 --> 00:25:45,460
It's the truth!

485
00:25:46,380 --> 00:25:48,260
There he is! Huh?

486
00:25:56,580 --> 00:26:00,051
Now which one of you people
has the nerve to call me weird?

487
00:26:02,900 --> 00:26:04,360
I'm just a normal guy.

488
00:26:05,740 --> 00:26:06,570
That's so.

489
00:26:06,653 --> 00:26:07,483
You look insane.

490
00:26:07,566 --> 00:26:08,580
Don't be silly.

491
00:26:08,820 --> 00:26:11,537
I'm just an ordinary
run-of-the-mill hypnotist.

492
00:26:11,620 --> 00:26:14,100
A hypnotist? Really? Awesome!

493
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:15,499
Show us something!

494
00:26:15,820 --> 00:26:17,080
Yeah, do something!

495
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:19,250
What?

496
00:26:20,220 --> 00:26:21,180
You fools!

497
00:26:21,440 --> 00:26:23,937
What makes you think that I
would want to reveal my masterful

498
00:26:24,020 --> 00:26:27,057
hypnosis techniques to a
bunch of roadside strangers?

499
00:26:27,140 --> 00:26:28,640
Okay, you convinced me.

500
00:26:28,860 --> 00:26:30,297
Now watch the ring closely.

501
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:31,210
What a ham.

502
00:26:31,360 --> 00:26:34,536
So, by the count of Django,
you'll be in a deep sleep.

503
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:36,033
Alright, are you ready?

504
00:26:37,540 --> 00:26:39,680
One, two, jungle!

505
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:45,460
Who is this freaky weirdo?

506
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:51,230
Hi!

507
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:53,692
What are you doing here?

508
00:26:53,820 --> 00:26:56,837
I came here to be alone, but
I guess that's not happening.

509
00:26:56,920 --> 00:26:58,097
Did you follow me?

510
00:26:58,180 --> 00:26:59,117
What do you want?

511
00:26:59,200 --> 00:27:00,800
Oh, you know, nothing.

512
00:27:03,640 --> 00:27:05,040
Oh, ouch.

513
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:14,557
I can't believe that butler.

514
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:16,464
Insulting my old man like that.

515
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:18,500
Yeah, I hate that guy too.

516
00:27:18,860 --> 00:27:22,040
No matter what anyone says,
Yasopp was a great pirate.

517
00:27:22,940 --> 00:27:23,770
Yeah, really.

518
00:27:23,853 --> 00:27:27,457
I've always looked up to my old man for
living every day with his life on the line,

519
00:27:27,540 --> 00:27:28,777
out in the open ocean.

520
00:27:28,860 --> 00:27:31,497
He made a brave choice
and I admire him for that.

521
00:27:31,580 --> 00:27:35,057
And then that stupid snotty
butler stomped all over my pride.

522
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:36,337
He's gonna pay for this.

523
00:27:36,420 --> 00:27:37,400
Hey, wait.

524
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:40,894
Do you know if you're ever
going to see that girl again?

525
00:27:41,380 --> 00:27:42,360
I don't know.

526
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:47,757
I'll consider it if that butler
apologizes to me for the things he said.

527
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:49,320
That butler right there?

528
00:27:50,060 --> 00:27:52,297
Yeah, that's the one. If he...

529
00:27:52,380 --> 00:27:54,637
What in the world is he doing down here?

530
00:27:54,720 --> 00:27:55,880
That's a big idea!

531
00:27:57,180 --> 00:28:01,680
Hey, Jango, I specifically ordered
you to keep yourself inconspicuous.

532
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:03,957
That doesn't mean sleeping in the road.

533
00:28:04,040 --> 00:28:05,400
Relax, man.

534
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:07,697
I've perfected the
skills of the chameleon.

535
00:28:07,780 --> 00:28:09,020
I blend in.

536
00:28:09,240 --> 00:28:12,037
There's someone else with
him, and man, is he weird.

537
00:28:12,120 --> 00:28:13,417
I've never seen him before.

538
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:14,330
Who is he?

539
00:28:15,400 --> 00:28:18,760
So, have all the necessary
preparations been made?

540
00:28:19,040 --> 00:28:20,100
But of course.

541
00:28:24,020 --> 00:28:25,980
We can start any time.

542
00:28:26,520 --> 00:28:28,680
On the rich girl murder plan.

543
00:28:32,280 --> 00:28:33,680
Murder?

544
00:28:58,680 --> 00:29:01,697
Before someone could beat me.

545
00:29:01,780 --> 00:29:05,897
The promised land was in my grasp.

546
00:29:05,980 --> 00:29:08,620
And I wouldn't let go.

547
00:29:09,440 --> 00:29:13,857
My only dream.

548
00:29:13,940 --> 00:29:18,977
I won't stop chasing

549
00:29:19,060 --> 00:29:21,780
it until it comes true.

550
00:29:23,300 --> 00:29:27,117
But if the world ever changes?

551
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:30,417
As I call out your name.

552
00:29:30,500 --> 00:29:34,077
We could return to the time.

553
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:36,920
When everything's the same.

554
00:29:37,620 --> 00:29:41,077
As long as I'm with you.

555
00:29:41,160 --> 00:29:43,920
My memories won't fade.

556
00:29:44,700 --> 00:29:49,260
Waiting for that one true dream?
